---
export const prerender = false;
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { getSupabaseAdmin } from "../../../lib/supabase";
import FaqAccordion from "../../../components/FaqAccordion.astro";

const fallbackFaqs = [
  { question: "Is vibe coding inherently insecure?", answer: "No, but unreviewed vibe coding often is. The risk isn't the AI itself, it's shipping AI-generated code the same way you'd ship code you fully understood and tested, when you haven't done either. The fix isn't avoiding AI tools, it's adding the review and testing step that any professional codebase already has." },
  { question: "How do you prevent these risks in a professional build?", answer: "Every line we ship goes through senior engineer review before it's merged, automated test suites covering the logic that matters, and a security pass on anything touching authentication, payments, or user data. AI writes the first draft faster; a person still signs off on what actually ships." },
  { question: "What should I check before shipping AI-generated code myself?", answer: "At minimum: search for hardcoded API keys or credentials before committing anything, check that user input is validated server-side (not just in the interface), confirm authentication actually blocks unauthorized access by testing it directly, and run the code past someone with real security experience if it will handle real user data." },
];

let faqItems = fallbackFaqs;

try {
  const supabase = getSupabaseAdmin();
  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from('faq_items')
    .select('question, answer, sort_order')
    .eq('section', 'vibe_coding_risks')
    .eq('published', true)
    .order('sort_order');

  if (!error && data && data.length > 0) {
    faqItems = data;
  }
} catch (err) {
  // Fallback to hardcoded content on error
}

const faqLdJson = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((f: { question: string; answer: string }) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.question,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.answer },
  })),
};

Astro.response.headers.set('Cache-Control', 's-maxage=60, stale-while-revalidate=300');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vibe Coding Risks & Security Checklist | Lenoo AI"
  description="The real security risks and limitations of vibe coding, honestly explained, plus a practical checklist before you ship AI-generated code."
  canonicalPath="/services/vibe-coding/risks-and-limitations"
  ogImage="/assets/og-services.jpg"
  ldJson={[faqLdJson]}
>

<header class="hero-section">
  <div class="container" style="text-align:center;">
    <div style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:rgba(0,227,253,0.12);border:1px solid rgba(0,227,253,0.28);border-radius:100px;padding:6px 16px;color:#a8f2ff;font-size:0.875rem;font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;justify-content:center;" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Home</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Services</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span><a href="/services/vibe-coding" style="color:inherit;text-decoration:none;opacity:0.65;">Vibe Coding</a><span style="opacity:0.4;padding:0 4px;"> / </span>Risks &amp; Limitations</div>
    <h1>Vibe Coding Risks, <span class="gradient-text">Honestly Explained.</span></h1>
    <p style="color:rgba(255,255,255,0.72); font-size:1.125rem; max-width:640px; margin:1.5rem auto 2.5rem; line-height:1.8;">
      AI-generated code isn't automatically dangerous, but shipping it unreviewed can be. Here's what actually goes wrong, why, and what to check before anything AI-generated goes live.
    </p>
    <div style="display:flex; gap:16px; justify-content:center; flex-wrap:wrap;">
      <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="vibe_risks_hero" class="btn-primary">Discuss Your Project</button>
      <a href="#checklist" class="btn-outline">See the Checklist ↓</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<div style="position:relative; overflow:hidden; height:260px;">
  <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1743090661053-3d1feb2beab7?w=1400&h=260&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Close-up of code on a screen during a code review" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" width="1400" height="260" style="width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 40%;" />
  <div style="position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top,rgba(0,19,32,0.45) 0%,transparent 55%),linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(0,104,117,0.20),rgba(0,37,59,0.45));"></div>
</div>

<!-- HONEST FRAMING -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="honest-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="split-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 id="honest-heading" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The Risk Isn't the AI. It's What Happens After.</h2>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
          Every horror story about vibe coding follows the same pattern: someone generated code with AI, it appeared to work, and it shipped without anyone checking whether it actually handled the cases that matter, bad input, unauthorized access, a database query that wasn't supposed to be public. That's not a new problem invented by AI. It's the same problem that happens whenever code ships without review, AI-generated or not. What's changed is that AI makes it faster and cheaper to generate a lot of code quickly, which also makes it faster and cheaper to skip the review step if nobody insists on it.
        </p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
          None of this means AI-generated code is inherently worse. In many cases it follows more consistent patterns than code written under deadline pressure by a tired developer. The risk is specifically in treating "it ran without an error" as proof that it's safe, which it isn't.
        </p>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
          There's also a scale effect worth naming directly. A single developer writing code by hand can only produce so much of it in a day, which naturally limits how much unreviewed risk enters a codebase at once. AI removes that natural speed limit. A team that used to generate a handful of risky patterns a week can now generate them in an afternoon, if nobody's checking. The fix isn't slowing down the AI, it's making sure the checking step scales up to match the new pace of output.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-media">
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1753450711825-b58801aee254?w=800&h=600&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop" alt="Developer debugging code, looking intently at a screen full of code" loading="lazy" width="800" height="600" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT ACTUALLY GOES WRONG -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="wrong-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="wrong-heading">What Actually Goes Wrong</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bento-grid bento-grid-2">
      <div class="card risks-card" data-risks-expand>
        <button type="button" class="risks-card-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="risks-panel-1" id="risks-toggle-1">
          <div>
            <div class="icon-box"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:24px;">key_off</span></div>
            <h3 style="font-weight:700; font-size:1.0625rem; color:#00253b;">Exposed Secrets</h3>
          </div>
          <span class="risks-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="risks-card-panel" id="risks-panel-1" role="region" aria-labelledby="risks-toggle-1" hidden>
          <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">API keys, database credentials, or tokens hardcoded directly into the code AI generated, then committed to a public repository, is one of the most common real incidents. It happens because the AI has no way to know which values are secrets unless the developer tells it, and automated scanners on platforms like GitHub often find these within minutes of a public push, which is exactly how fast a leaked key can be abused.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card risks-card" data-risks-expand>
        <button type="button" class="risks-card-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="risks-panel-2" id="risks-toggle-2">
          <div>
            <div class="icon-box"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:24px;">gpp_bad</span></div>
            <h3 style="font-weight:700; font-size:1.0625rem; color:#00253b;">Injection Vulnerabilities</h3>
          </div>
          <span class="risks-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="risks-card-panel" id="risks-panel-2" role="region" aria-labelledby="risks-toggle-2" hidden>
          <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Database queries or commands built directly from user input without sanitization. AI can write either safe or unsafe versions of the same query depending on how the request was framed, and won't necessarily flag the difference unless asked to, since both versions look equally correct to something that isn't specifically checking for this pattern.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card risks-card" data-risks-expand>
        <button type="button" class="risks-card-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="risks-panel-3" id="risks-toggle-3">
          <div>
            <div class="icon-box"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:24px;">lock_open</span></div>
            <h3 style="font-weight:700; font-size:1.0625rem; color:#00253b;">Broken Access Control</h3>
          </div>
          <span class="risks-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="risks-card-panel" id="risks-panel-3" role="region" aria-labelledby="risks-toggle-3" hidden>
          <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">An interface that hides a button for users without permission, while the underlying request still works if called directly. It looks secure in the browser and isn't. This is the single most common pattern behind "AI-built app leaked user data" stories, because it's invisible during normal use and only shows up when someone deliberately tries to bypass the interface.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card risks-card" data-risks-expand>
        <button type="button" class="risks-card-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="risks-panel-4" id="risks-toggle-4">
          <div>
            <div class="icon-box"><span class="material-symbols-outlined" aria-hidden="true" style="color:#006875; font-size:24px;">layers</span></div>
            <h3 style="font-weight:700; font-size:1.0625rem; color:#00253b;">Silent Technical Debt</h3>
          </div>
          <span class="risks-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="risks-card-panel" id="risks-panel-4" role="region" aria-labelledby="risks-toggle-4" hidden>
          <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Working code that's structured in a way nobody, including the person who "wrote" it, can easily maintain six months later. AI optimizes for a working answer to the immediate question, not necessarily for a codebase that stays healthy as it grows, so the cost of this one often doesn't show up until someone tries to add a feature and can't tell why everything breaks.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- HORROR STORY PATTERN -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="pattern-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">
    <h2 id="pattern-heading" style="text-align:left; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The Pattern Behind Most Horror Stories</h2>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Strip away the specifics and most publicly shared "vibe coding disaster" stories follow the same three steps. First, someone builds something fast and it works in every scenario they personally tested. Second, it ships, often to real users, without anyone outside the builder reviewing it. Third, someone else, a curious user, a security researcher, an attacker, tests a scenario the builder never thought to try, and finds the gap.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Notice that none of the three steps require the AI to have "made a mistake" in any unusual sense. Steps one and two are exactly how a lot of legitimate fast-moving projects work. Step three is what a code review or a security pass exists to catch before it happens in public instead of in a controlled test. The stories aren't evidence that AI-generated code is uniquely dangerous. They're evidence of what happens whenever review gets skipped, at a volume and speed unreviewed AI output makes newly easy to reach.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      What makes these stories spread is also worth naming honestly: they're more interesting to write about than the much larger number of AI-assisted projects that shipped fine because someone reviewed them properly. A dramatic breach makes a headline. A boring, well-reviewed launch doesn't. That selection effect makes the risk feel larger and more universal than it actually is, without making the underlying lesson, review your output, any less true.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHO'S MOST AT RISK -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="atrisk-heading">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="atrisk-heading">Who's Actually Most at Risk</h2>
      <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem;">The risk profile changes depending on who's building and what's at stake.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="builds-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Solo Non-Technical Founders</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Highest exposure, since there's often no one else in the loop to catch a mistake before real users encounter it. Not a reason to avoid building, a reason to get a second set of eyes before launch.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Small Teams Moving Fast</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Moderate exposure. A second developer reviewing changes catches a lot, but speed pressure often means review gets skipped exactly when it matters most, right before a deadline.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Established Companies</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Lower exposure if existing code review and security processes are applied to AI-generated code the same way they're applied to everything else. Risk rises sharply wherever that process gets waived for AI-written code specifically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Anyone Handling Payments or Health Data</h3>
        <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:0.875rem; line-height:1.7;">Highest stakes regardless of team size. A mistake here isn't just embarrassing, it can be financially or legally serious, which is exactly why review isn't optional in these categories.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- CHECKLIST -->
<section id="checklist" class="section" aria-labelledby="checklist-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">
    <h2 id="checklist-heading" style="text-align:left; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">A Practical Checklist Before You Ship</h2>
    <ul style="color:#42474e; font-size:1rem; line-height:2; padding-left:1.25rem;">
      <li><strong>Search the codebase for hardcoded secrets</strong> (API keys, passwords, tokens) before any commit, and confirm none are in version control history either.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm validation happens server-side</strong>, not only in the interface. A user can bypass anything that only lives in the browser.</li>
      <li><strong>Test access control by actually trying to break it</strong>: log in as a lower-permission user and attempt the higher-permission action directly, not just through the visible interface.</li>
      <li><strong>Check for rate limiting</strong> on anything public-facing, especially login and any form that touches an external API you pay for.</li>
      <li><strong>Read the code that handles money, auth, or personal data</strong> line by line, even if you skim everything else. These three categories are where mistakes cost the most.</li>
      <li><strong>Run it past someone with real security experience</strong> before it handles real user data, even a single external review catches patterns a first-time builder won't recognize.</li>
      <li><strong>Check what happens when something fails.</strong> A payment that times out, an upload that's the wrong file type, a network call that drops, confirm the app fails safely instead of in a way that corrupts data or grants unintended access.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep a record of what changed and why.</strong> Even a simple changelog makes it far easier to spot when a new AI-generated change quietly altered something unrelated to what was asked for.</li>
      <li><strong>Set a reminder to revisit this list on every major feature</strong>, not just once at launch. A codebase that was safe at version one doesn't stay safe automatically as new features get added on top of it.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHEN NOT TO -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f0f3ff;" aria-labelledby="not-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px;">
    <h2 id="not-heading" style="text-align:left; margin-bottom:1.5rem;">When Vibe Coding Isn't the Right Approach</h2>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Unsupervised, uninspected vibe coding is a poor fit for anything handling payments, health information, legal documents, or access control for sensitive systems, exactly the cases where a quiet mistake causes real damage. It's also a poor fit for large, long-lived codebases with no one reviewing structure over time, since speed without oversight compounds into a mess faster than manual development would. None of that means AI has no place in these projects. It means the review and testing layer becomes non-negotiable rather than optional.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85;">
      A useful rule of thumb: the more expensive a mistake would be to discover after the fact, in money, in trust, in someone's personal data being exposed, the less appropriate it is to skip review, no matter how well the demo worked. That's not a statement about AI. It's the same rule that applied to manually written software long before AI coding tools existed; AI just made it easier to forget.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- BRIDGE / MID CTA -->
<section class="section" aria-labelledby="next-step-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:820px; text-align:center;">
    <h2 id="next-step-heading" style="margin-bottom:1rem;">This Is Exactly Why We Review Every Line</h2>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
      Every risk above is a review problem, not an AI problem. That's why our process pairs AI-accelerated development with senior engineer review, automated testing, and a security pass on anything touching auth, payments, or user data, on every project, every time.
    </p>
    <p style="color:#42474e; font-size:1.0625rem; line-height:1.85; margin-bottom:2rem;">
      You get the speed of vibe coding without skipping the step that prevents these exact stories, and without having to become a security expert yourself to know it's been handled.
    </p>
    <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="vibe_risks_mid" class="btn-primary">Discuss Your Project</button>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="section" style="background:#f9f9ff;" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:720px;">
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    </div>
    <FaqAccordion items={faqItems} />
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FINAL CTA -->
<section class="section-sm" aria-label="Call to action">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="cta-band">
      <div style="position:relative; z-index:1; text-align:center;">
        <p class="cta-eyebrow">Free Discovery Call</p>
        <h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem); margin-bottom:1rem;">Want It Built Right the First Time?</h2>
        <p style="font-size:1.125rem; max-width:36rem; margin:0 auto 2rem; line-height:1.7;">Tell us what you're building. We'll give you a timeline and cost estimate, free, with no commitment.</p>
        <div class="cta-trust-badges">
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ 100% free</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ No commitment</span>
          <span class="cta-trust-badge">✓ Refund guarantee</span>
        </div>
        <button onclick="openModal(this)" data-source="vibe_risks_final" class="btn-primary" style="font-size:1.125rem; padding:1.125rem 2.75rem;">Discuss Your Project</button>
        <p style="font-size:.875rem; margin-top:1rem; color:#94a3b8;">30-min call · No sales pressure</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

